      "For food retailers and independent store owners, accurate stock management is the foundation of a profitable business. Yet most small store owners have a chaotic relationship with their inventory: they know roughly what they have, they can feel when something is running low, but they rarely have a precise, real-time picture of what is on their shelves, what is about to expire, and what has quietly passed its date unnoticed. This disconnect between perceived and actual inventory is the root cause of most expiry losses.",
      "A stock audit is a systematic process of physically counting and verifying your inventory against your records. For food retailers, audits serve two purposes simultaneously: they confirm stock quantities and they allow for a systematic expiry date check of everything on the shelves. For complete inventory rotation principles, see our [FEFO Inventory Management Guide](/blog/fefo-inventory-management).",
      "## The Action Plan",
      "Before conducting any stock audit, you need a clear framework for what to do when you find near-expiry or expired stock. Create three categories: products with more than 30 days remaining (safe), products with 7–30 days remaining (urgent — apply markdown or initiate supplier return), and products with less than 7 days or already expired (remove immediately and log for disposal). Check date calculations quickly with our free [Expiry Calculator](/expiry-calculator).",
      "## Types of Stock Audit: Full Counts vs Cycle Counts",
      "A **full stock audit** counts everything in the store at once. It gives a complete accuracy picture and suits year-end reporting, but it disrupts trading and is usually done after hours. **Cycle counting** splits the catalogue into small groups counted on a rotating schedule — dairy on Monday, pharmacy lines on Tuesday, and so on. For expiry control, cycle counting wins: dated categories get checked every week instead of twice a year, so short-dated stock surfaces while it can still be sold, marked down, or returned. Most food retailers combine both — weekly cycle counts for perishables and a full audit once or twice a year.",
      "## How to Run an Expiry-Focused Stock Audit, Step by Step",
      "1. **Prepare the list.** Pull every SKU in the category with its recorded batch and expiry dates before anyone touches a shelf.\n2. **Count and compare.** Record physical quantity per batch, and check the actual printed expiry date against the system date — mismatches are where losses hide.\n3. **Flag by remaining life.** Sort findings into buckets: expired (remove immediately), under 30 days (markdown or promote now), 30–90 days (watch list), and healthy.\n4. **Investigate discrepancies.** A batch on the shelf that is not in the system means receiving skipped a step; a batch in the system but not on the shelf usually means unrecorded damage, theft, or a picking error.\n5. **Act the same day.** An audit that ends in a spreadsheet changes nothing. Markdowns, transfers, and supplier returns should be triggered before the team leaves.\n6. **Record the write-off value.** Tracking the money value of expired stock per category, every cycle, is what turns audits from a chore into a management tool.",
      "## Stock Audit Checklist for Food Retailers",
