      "## How FEFO Rotation Fits Into Auditing",
      "An audit tells you where you stand; rotation keeps you there. During every count, re-shelf each bay in [FEFO order](/blog/fefo-inventory-management) — earliest expiry forward — so the audit physically fixes the shelf as it checks it. Stores that only count, without rotating, re-discover the same expired products next cycle. Pair the audit with the weekly routine in the [7-step plan to reduce expiry losses](/blog/reducing-expiry-losses) and the expired bucket shrinks cycle over cycle.",
      "## The Numbers Worth Tracking",
      "Four figures tell you whether your audit programme works: **write-off value per month** (the money thrown away), **expiry loss percentage** (write-offs divided by category sales), **stock accuracy** (SKUs where system and shelf agree), and **near-expiry recovery rate** (short-dated stock sold at markdown instead of binned). Review them monthly, by category. A category whose losses refuse to fall usually has a buying problem — order quantities too large for its sales rate — not a shelf problem.",
